messages.body_plain:
I have a time varying, factor analysis model with a shrinkage parameter that I am estimating.  The model that I have runs, but is very slow.  Any thoughts about how to speed it up?
The data are a panel with 5 issues(j), 195 individuals, and 16 time periods.  The shrinkage parameter is phi.

Model is Y_ijt=betat[j,t]_Yijt-1+sigma
beta[j,t]=normal(phi[j,t]*beta[j,t][t-1]+(1-phi1[j,t])*gamma[t],pow(tau[j][1],-2));

To reduce the parameters, I constrained tau to be the same for each issue.

I wasn't sure if there was a way to make the y1,y2,y3,y4,y5 into an array (j,t,n) and if that would speed it up.  I didn't see any factor analysis models that might address this issue in the examples.
Obviously, there are ways to try to tackle the shrinkage parameter with pooling (although it is less clear how to do that with a factor analysis model), but I was hoping I could get this model to work
since it provides a closer fit to the problem I working on.

Thanks,
Greg
Model:
panel1<-"
data {
  int<lower=0> N; ##respondents, 195
  int<lower=0> T; ##time 16
  real y1[N,T]; ##5 meaaures per individual
  real y2[N,T];
  real y3[N,T];
  real y4[N,T];
  real y5[N,T];
}
parameters {
  real beta1[T];
  real beta2[T];
  real beta3[T];
  real beta4[T];
  real beta5[T];
  real gamma[T];
 real<lower=0> tau[5];
real phi1[T];
 real phi2[T];
 real phi3[T];
 real phi4[T];
 real phi5[T];
vector<lower=0>[5]sigma;

}

model
{
sigma ~ inv_gamma(.1,0.001);

gamma ~ normal(0,100);

beta1 ~ normal(0,100);
beta2 ~ normal(0,100);
beta3 ~ normal(0,100);
beta4 ~ normal(0,100);
beta5 ~ normal(0,100);
phi1 ~ normal(0,10);
phi2 ~ normal(0,10);
phi3 ~ normal(0,10);
phi4 ~ normal(0,10);
phi5 ~ normal(0,10);

tau ~ inv_gamma(.1,0.01);

for (t in 2:T)  {
beta1[t] ~normal(phi1[t]*beta1[t-1]+(1-phi1[t])*gamma[t],pow(tau[1],-2));
beta2[t] ~normal(phi2[t]*beta2[t-1]+(1-phi2[t])*gamma[t],pow(tau[2],-2));
beta3[t] ~normal(phi3[t]*beta3[t-1]+(1-phi3[t])*gamma[t],pow(tau[3],-2));
beta4[t] ~normal(phi4[t]*beta4[t-1]+(1-phi4[t])*gamma[t],pow(tau[4],-2));
beta5[t] ~normal(phi5[t]*beta5[t-1]+(1-phi5[t])*gamma[t],pow(tau[5],-2));
}

for(n in 1:N){
for (t in 2:T){
real mu1[N,T];
real mu2[N,T];
real mu3[N,T];
real mu4[N,T];
real mu5[N,T];

mu1[n,t]<-beta1[t]*y1[n,t-1];
y1[n,t] ~ normal(mu1[n,t], pow(sigma[1],-2));

mu2[n,t]<-beta2[t]*y2[n,t-1];
y2[n,t] ~ normal(mu2[n,t], pow(sigma[2],-2));

mu3[n,t]<-beta3[t]*y3[n,t-1];
y3[n,t] ~ normal(mu3[n,t], pow(sigma[3],-2));

mu4[n,t]<-beta4[t]*y4[n,t-1];
y4[n,t] ~ normal(mu4[n,t], pow(sigma[4],-2));

mu5[n,t]<-beta5[t]*y5[n,t-1];
y5[n,t] ~ normal(mu5[n,t], pow(sigma[5],-2));

}
}
}
"

On Friday, August 2, 2013 2:54:13 PM UTC-4, Sylvain Bonhommeau wrote:
> i'm trying to run the "Eight schools" example on a cluster computer using Rmpi (before i start with my code). My problem is that the stan code is apparently compiled on each node in the /tmp but this is limited to 100Mo and then it crashes.

In general, with the parallel stuff, you want to compile the model once, pass that stanfit object to each of the workers, and collect the output with the sflist2stanfit() function. There help page for sflist2stanfit has an example. In short, you want to do

> library(rstan)
> library(foreach)
> cl <- makeCluster()
> print(clusterCall(cl, function() Sys.info()))
>
> schools_code <- '
>   data {
>     int<lower=0> J; // number of schools
>     real y[J]; // estimated treatment effects
>     real<lower=0> sigma[J]; // s.e. of effect estimates
>   }
>   parameters {
>     real mu;
>     real<lower=0> tau;
>     real eta[J];
>   }
>   transformed parameters {
>     real theta[J];
>       for (j in 1:J)
>       theta[j] <- mu + tau * eta[j];
>     }
>   model {
>     eta ~ normal(0, 1);
>     y ~ normal(theta, sigma);
>   }
> '
>
> schools_dat <- list(J = 8,
> y = c(28, 8, -3, 7, -1, 1, 18, 12),
> sigma = c(15, 10, 16, 11, 9, 11, 10, 18))

> fit <- stan(model_code = schools_code, data = schools_dat, chains = 0) # create an empty stanfit object

>
>
> a <- foreach(i=1:16) %dopar% {
> library(rstan)

> stan(fit = fit, iter = 1000, chain_id = i, seed = 12345) # update empty object 16 ways
> }
> a <- sflist2stanfit(a) # create stanfit object with 16 chains
> stopCluster(cl)

Ben

great, it works like this! thanks for these quick answers. I will also check with the tempdir option and i'll keep you posted if i have something.

thanks a lot!

sylvain

